---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import DebuggingChecklist from '../../components/DebuggingChecklist.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Systematic Debugging Checklist" description="A structured checklist for debugging — reproduce, isolate, fix, and verify.">
  <DebuggingChecklist client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why "just try things" wastes time</h2>
  <p>Debugging without a system leads to randomly changing code and hoping — which occasionally works but rarely builds understanding of the actual root cause, meaning similar bugs keep resurfacing. A structured approach (reproduce, isolate, fix, verify) is slower per-step but faster overall.</p>

  <h2>How to use this checklist</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Work through each phase in order rather than jumping straight to guessing fixes.</li>
    <li>Don't skip "reproduce" — an unreliable reproduction makes every later step unreliable too.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Jumping straight to changing code</strong> before confirming you can reliably reproduce the issue.</li>
    <li><strong>Fixing the first plausible cause found</strong> without verifying it's actually the root cause, sometimes masking the real issue instead of fixing it.</li>
    <li><strong>Not checking for similar bugs elsewhere</strong> once a root cause is found — the same mistake pattern often exists in more than one place.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Working through a hard-to-reproduce production bug systematically</li>
    <li>Teaching a junior developer a structured debugging approach</li>
    <li>Avoiding "shotgun debugging" under time pressure during an incident</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What if I can't reproduce the bug at all?</strong> Focus on gathering more context first — logs, error messages, and the exact conditions reported — rather than guessing at fixes for a bug you can't observe directly.</p>
</ToolLayout>
